 you could get  interesting results  you know we see on our site we have  we have 60 million students use the site  every month  we want them to interact with each other  we want them to answer their questions  uh with that and and that's incredibly  valuable for someone who maybe does not  have someone else to interact with they  don't have access to a kind of a rich  environment  but it i think the technology would have  to go a very very long way to come close  to what you could get with a very rich  classroom environment that's led by an  inspiring teacher but when i say lead  not being lectured by an inspiring  teacher but really mentored and coached  by an inspiring teacher  given that these are a different set of  skills from teachers from what teachers  have been really brought up in  do you have a vision for how to kind of  get to  the sort of 21st century flipped  classroom instructor that we would need  to do this  yeah and it's an open question i i don't  know 100 i mean it's actually exciting  to be here to at a school that is so  focused on education i i i suspect a lot  of the answer might be for for the the  future teachers to experience it  themselves for them to understand it and  for them to viscerally feel the  difference between being lectured at and  being able to engage with the content  and have conversations with their peers  and discuss it at a deeper level  but i think we're just we're at the very  early stages i mean in my opinion or  it seems like a lot of the adoption's  happening faster than i would have  guessed uh we're being used in twenty  thousand classrooms right now and this  is really just over the last couple of  years and we have two people who engage  directly with classrooms we're a very  small organization so it seems like  there's a hunger out there and a lot of  the teachers we talk to this is kind of  the direction they've always wanted to  be in they've wanted to they got into  the profession because they wanted to  connect with students they wanted to you  know they wanted to be that teacher that  takes that student from you know a  certain attitude to a were they taking  ownership of their life and and they  found it difficult when they have 30  faces that they have to kind of lecture  on right  um  and  one thing that i've heard  as sort of a concern from from teachers  is that there might be a um  well i i maybe i'll put it this way  the concerns have not come just from  teachers but  that online learning often gets sold as  a cost saving you know as something that  okay well we we public education is very  expensive what can we do to save money  do you think it is  a viable way to do education less  expensively  i i kind of i  i don't know um is is a simple answer i  mean i know for my own kids i don't want  them online only and i and i you know  the way i view it if education was  perfect uh we should think about how we  can then you know if we're making a  great car we can then think about how to  streamline the factory or whatever we're  doing but uh you know the reality and  you know there's a million alarmist  articles that come out about all the  reasons why we should worry about  education and so in my mind i would not  try to de-resource the problem i would  try to maybe hold resources constantly  maybe even potentially increase them but  try to get  but try to have better outcomes and so i  view technology as much more of given  whatever resources you have and you know  it's hard to argue that you're ever  putting too much too many resources in  education given whatever resources you  have how do we actually optimize  outcomes  um  you've had a good time doing the videos  that you i mean i don't think you'd do  as  3 000 of them if you didn't like doing  them yes no i i like doing them a lot  yes  do you have any particular favorites  that you  were sort of like  you know you've heard or have you heard  from people who are particularly fond of  a certain video oh you know there's a  bunch i mean well i think one of you  know people ask me like what's the trick  how do you yeah and and i say well  the video that i'm doing i try to  convince myself that's my favorite video  so that you literally have like you know  you hear the energy you hear the  excitement and you can't understate how  important those cues are for the student  if the lecturer themselves  does not care about it the the student  immediately we're very sensitive to that  if you're saying the next step in this  equation i mean who's going to care  about that and so i think it's very  important for the lecturer themselves to  feel excited that they get to do this um  but there's all sorts of topics i i'm a  real big fan of the cosmology videos it  was a reason for me to learn a lot of  cosmology and really understand it and  it's something that always fascinated me  um i i but i get into the finance videos  my old life i was an analyst at an  investment firm and obviously there's a  certain part of me that gets very  passionate about that i i love doing the  history videos i love doing i mean you  know i like i like doing it all  you get some pushback from educators who  are like well he didn't go to education  school he didn't you know do you feel  like  maybe teaching is not as hard as people  uh  make it up to be  no well no i actually isaac i think it's  probably harder than people make it out  to be  and you know depending on what you call  a teacher it's you know  you can determine whether i am one or  whether i'm a content creator i  don't have to or i mean depending on how  you do it you know i don't have 30 live  students that i'm trying to cater to  that i'm you know some of them might  have some problem at home some of them  have some issue some are strug you know  that is a  that is a nuanced and deep and important  role that i am not doing when i just  make a nice resource when i make this  this nice video hopefully it's an  engaging video it gets people excited  about the subject matter and and you  know i want to stress this you know i  recently uh met wendy kopp at dinner the  founder of teach for america  and she was telling me that the single  biggest determinant of the teachers that  they have and they study this very  carefully what are the teachers that are  showing outside outsized gains and most  people think it's going to be content  delivery and there is some correlation  if someone can deliver a good lecture  then their students tend to do well but  the one the thing that that really moves  a dial is a teacher's ability to change  the students mindsets that to make the  students realize that they really have  to take ownership of their learning that  they really have to start thinking about  their futures and and  when she said that i was like well i  need to quote you and i'm quoting her  right now  because that's very consistent what  we're saying instead of five or ten  percent of a teacher's time being that  that that coach that inspiration and  ninety percent being the the content  delivery we want a hundred percent of a  teacher's time being that coach that  inspiration that that that person in the  student's life that can change their  their mindset they can change their  outlook uh that can be that mentor and  that's something i'll never be able to  do as a video creator  and you need a live human being for  do you think there's much of a stretch  between  standing in front of a group of 25  students and doing an exercise at the  board versus doing an exercise in a  youtube video that's going to be seen at  home  you know we've been we're and we're  constantly looking for folks to make  more and more videos and obviously  there's some incredible teachers out  there many many incredible teachers but  we found that um  there's a certain nervousness once you  record yourself and you put it out there  for the world to see and comment on um  and so yes there there is a difference  when you i mean i actually don't think  about it when i make a video i kind of  just imagine my cousin's listening and i  think it just makes it more intimate and  warm hopefully but uh we have seen folks  who are i think amazing teachers in the  in physical form but when they know  they're being recorded uh they tend to  get a little nervous  and i think teachers are  accustomed to making relationships or  forming relationships with students that  they have right in front of them and  there's  this uncertainty around well how am i  relating to how are relating how are  students relating to me  when they see me on their computer  and is that obviously you started with  your cousin so i don't know if you ever  sort of went through that period of  uncertainty yeah no i go through that  period of uncertainty every time i  upload a video and and this is what's  exciting these days is i can upload a  video  now and within an hour i'll get 50  comments from kids all of all ages i  shouldn't say just kids all over the  world and uh it matters to me i mean it  really helps me if a few say wow that  really helped that was exactly what i  needed i've gotten through that concept  now and so in that way i get much more  direct feedback you know i've i've done  i've never been a a professional teacher  but i've done some teaching in college  and you know i used to give a lecture or  teach a class and the day would go but  no feedback i'd get i'd get insecure  and and that would happen and maybe at  the end of the term you might get one or  two students if you know if you're lucky  who might say oh that was really helpful  you know thank you um but you don't get  that direct feedback as as often and and  you know in online you get that feedback  for better or worse i mean sometimes the  feedback can be harsh but once again  that that can only make you better  would you expect every instructor to  produce their own content or do you  think that there could be just a  standardized repository that the  teachers would draw from i i think both  i mean i'd like to see a world where  every uh instructor if they're not happy  with what they're seeing out in the  world that they are creating their own  if they hey i could do better than that  i you know that that person's not  covering this nuance or this angle let  let me do something uh with that said i  i can't imagine over time the the  content delivery you know just as every  teacher is not writing their own  textbook that you kind of start to  settle on it becomes reasonably resource  intensive you know now we can actually  use data and analytics to figure out  which of the the the videos and once  again i think the videos are much in  some ways they're analogous to the text  in a textbook but we can start to  measure how effective is it how engaging  is it and uh i could you know in five or  ten years  we won't need thousands of  explanations of factoring a polynomial  we could have a handful that have proven  themselves to to really engage students  and and uh resonate with them i have one  last question  okay  um  which is the the level of of work that  this type of education requires as  opposed to what teachers are accustomed  to doing now  what i've heard from one instructor who  does sort of a hybrid kind of you know  does a little bit of both he's like well  this the classes that have an online  component you really have to develop all  those online pieces in advance and then  you still have all of the work of the  you know regular interaction with  students  in short it sounds like it is more work  it may be worth it but do you think that  it is more work and perhaps worth it or  what's your view on that  what we're seeing is yeah i think today  in most schools especially i would say  public schools  where a teacher wants to do something in  this direction  it is more work for them today because  to some degree they have to serve two  masters they have to serve the  traditional master of this is the state  curriculum you have to do this in this  week but the same time they want to have  the flexibility and  and allow the students to work at their  own pace and cater to the students so it  is more work for them today  it's less work than if they wanted to do  a a student-centered curriculum 10 years  ago without any technology  with that said the direction that  everything seems to be going in is that  the tools are getting better you know  khan academy itself is nowhere near  being a perfect tool  we have a list of 200 things that we  need to fix about it make it easier for  teachers to use make it easier for them  to plan their curriculum  we have to add a lot more content so  that people don't have to go and fill in  the gaps in other places there's other  people making more and more tools so i  suspect as the tools get better as uh as  as kind of the the curriculum becomes  clearer i think this adoption of the  common core is actually a huge  opportunity  because states have adopted it it hasn't  kind of fully gone into implementation  yet  i think all of these things are kind of  converged the technology is going to  become more usable and more accessible  right now teachers have to struggle with  trying to get the laptop cart for their  class at the right period hopefully a  lot of those things are going to solve  themselves over the next four or five  years so yes it's i would say it's  harder today but the teachers think it's  worth it uh but i would suspect five ten  years from now it might be easier than a  traditional model okay  well thanks i should probably wrap up  now i could ask you lots more questions  oh no no no great great yeah that's  great questions i appreciate it
 

